# Copy to `apps/web/.env.local` and you are done:
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env.local
#
# **This file lives in `apps/web`, not at the repo root, because that is where
# Next reads it from.** `next dev` loads `.env.local` from its own project
# directory; a root one would be ignored in silence, which is the worst way for
# a configuration file to be wrong.
#
# **Development-tier values only, and that is a rule rather than a habit.**
# `turbo.json` declares `.env*` a `build` input, so anything written here is
# hashed into the build cache and travels with a remote-cached artifact. A
# production URL in this file is one `git add` from an incident (spec 0002, C13).
# Real credentials live in `fly secrets` — `docs/policy/security.md` ->
# `secret-store`.
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored. This file is committed, and holds no secret.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The database. `pnpm db:up` starts what these point at; see `docker-compose.yaml`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pooled. What the application uses, through PgBouncer in transaction-pooling
# mode — the same mode PlanetScale's pooler runs in (spec 0002, DD2). Session
# state does not survive between transactions on this connection, and that is
# the point of it being here rather than pointing straight at 5432.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://recomencemos:recomencemos@localhost:6432/recomencemos

# Direct. Migrations only. DDL, long transactions and anything session-scoped
# cannot run through a transaction pooler, so `@repo/domain/migrate` takes this
# one and the runtime client takes the pooled one above.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://recomencemos:recomencemos@localhost:5432/recomencemos

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notifications. `@repo/notifications` is the seam; two transports implement it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# **Which implementation of the seam runs.** `terminal` or `resend`.
#
# `terminal` is the development inbox: instead of delivering, it prints the
# notification — the magic link first, on a line of its own — to the terminal
# running `pnpm dev`, so you copy the link from there and never need a real
# mailbox. It is a second transport rather than a mode, which is why no call
# site knows it exists.
#
# **It refuses to exist outside `development` and `test`**, so it cannot end up
# on a deploy quietly swallowing everyone's mail.
#
# There is no default and an unset value throws. Neither answer is safe in both
# places: `resend` by default mails a stranger from an unwarmed domain on your
# first sign-in loop, and `terminal` by default makes a real deploy send nothing
# at all. Production sets `resend` through `fly secrets`.
NOTIFICATIONS_TRANSPORT=terminal

# **The kill switch.** Set it to anything and every send is refused, logged, and
# never delivered — one variable and a restart rather than a deploy, because a
# send is the one irreversible act in this system (DD10, DD14).
#
# Empty, `off`, `false`, `0` and `no` leave sending on. *Everything else engages
# it*, a typo included: the failure worth designing against is a switch someone
# believes is on and isn't.
#
# It is `off` here because the line above already makes development safe — with
# the terminal transport there is nothing to be irreversible about, and a
# fresh clone should be able to run the sign-in loop end to end. Engage it when
# you point a development machine at `resend`.
NOTIFICATIONS_KILL_SWITCH=off

# Who the mail is from.
#
# **This is the real, verified sending subdomain**, not a placeholder — Resend is
# configured for `mail.recomencemos.online` and its SPF, DKIM and DMARC records
# resolve (runbook §4). It is written here rather than left as `example.test`
# because neither half is a secret, and because §4 wants every send a developer
# makes to count toward the domain's warm-up curve instead of going nowhere.
# `RESEND_API_KEY` is still absent from this file and must stay absent.
#
# **Not `noreply@`**, which is still DD14's rule: the local part names someone.
# There is no `NOTIFICATIONS_REPLY_TO` beside it, and that is the amendment
# rather than an omission — the subdomain is send-only in Resend, so it publishes
# no MX record and nothing receives. A `Reply-To` pointing at a mailbox that does
# not exist is a dead end asserted; without one, a reply bounces and she is told.
# The variable returns the day a mailbox does.
#
# `senderIdentity()` throws rather than invent a value, and the `terminal`
# transport never reads this at all.
NOTIFICATIONS_FROM=Recomencemos <hola@mail.recomencemos.online>

# `RESEND_API_KEY` is deliberately **absent from this file, and must stay absent**.
# It is a credential, so it lives in `fly secrets` and in the operator's password
# manager (runbook §1, NFR24) — and `turbo.json` declares `.env*` a `build` input,
# so a key written here is hashed into the build cache and travels with a
# remote-cached artifact. To send from a development machine, put it in
# `.env.local`, which is gitignored.
